{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE PROVEN — the test's workers race on a SHARED INDEX; the contention seam is not at fault\n\nReproduced deterministically. This is a test-design defect, not a violation of the 9305\ncontention contract.\n\n### First: the CI evidence was being misread\n\nThe worker is a `python -c` program. Counting its lines:\n\n    1  import sys\n    2  sys.path.insert(0, sys.argv[3])\n    3  from rebar._store import gitutil\n    4  repo, name = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\n    5  open(f'{repo}/{name}.txt', 'w').write(name)\n    6  r = gitutil.run_git_write(repo, 'add', '--', f'{name}.txt')\n    7  assert r.returncode == 0, r.stderr\n    8  r = gitutil.run_git_write(repo, 'commit', '-q', '--no-verify', '-m', name)\n    9  assert r.returncode == 0, r.stderr\n\nBoth cited failures report `File \"<string>\", line 9` — so the failing inner command is\n**`git commit`**, not `git add`. Both also produce a **message-less** `AssertionError`, i.e.\n`r.stderr` was the empty string.\n\n### The mechanism\n\n`_with_index_lock_retry` takes the per-store advisory lock around **each** `run_git_write`\ncall, but the worker's add and commit are two separate calls — the PAIR is not serialized. So\nthis interleaving is legal and occurs under load:\n\n    alpha: add alpha.txt   -> index holds alpha.txt\n    beta:  add beta.txt    -> index holds alpha.txt AND beta.txt\n    alpha: commit          -> bare `git commit` commits the WHOLE index (both files); index clean\n    beta:  commit          -> nothing staged -> \"nothing to commit\" -> rc=1\n\n`git commit -q` writes \"nothing to commit, working tree clean\" to **stdout** and leaves\n**stderr empty** — which is precisely the empty-stderr rc=1 seen in CI.\n\n### Deterministic reproduction\n\nForcing that interleaving through the real seam:\n\n    alpha add    rc=0\n    beta  add    rc=0\n    alpha commit rc=0\n    beta  commit rc=1\n    beta  commit stderr=''\n    beta  commit stdout='On branch main\\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\\n'\n\n    git log:  5315cd2 alpha / 9471f2e seed\n    alpha in log: True   beta in log: False\n\n    HEAD contents:  5315cd2 alpha\n      alpha.txt | 1 +\n      beta.txt  | 1 +\n\nNote the last block: alpha's commit **swept up beta.txt**. Both writers' DATA lands and\nreachability is preserved — only beta's *commit* is a no-op. The test's `assert cp.returncode\n== 0` for both writers asserts something the product never promised: that two bare whole-index\ncommits against one repo are both non-empty.\n\nThe test also flakes locally on this checkout at roughly 1 in 25-65 runs of the single test,\nmatching the CI rate.\n\n### What is RULED OUT\n\n- **Not retry classification / exhaustion.** rc=1 with empty stderr never reaches\n  `_augment_lock_exhaustion`; no lock signature was involved at all.\n- **Not runner resource exhaustion.** All failing job logs across the cited runs were grepped\n  for `No space`, `ENOSPC`, `Cannot allocate`, and runner warnings: zero hits.\n- **Not a product contention defect.** Every git invocation through the seam behaved correctly;\n  git itself correctly reports an empty commit.\n\n### Fix direction (test-only, behavior preserving)\n\n1. **Observability first (AC #1).** The worker asserts on `r.stderr` alone, discarding both the\n   git stdout that carries the actual diagnosis and the identity of the failing inner command.\n   Each assertion should name its command and include stdout as well as stderr, so a future\n   failure is self-diagnosing rather than a bare `AssertionError`.\n2. **Remove the shared-index race** by having each worker commit its own pathspec, so a writer\n   that loses the interleaving still has something of its own to commit. That preserves the\n   real oracle — two concurrent writers through `run_git_write` against one repository both\n   succeed and both commits land — without weakening it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786500516051591001,"uuid":"9f45da16-6bc5-48d7-84c1-f8be5a1ec0e8"}